## The script

**0:00–0:20 · Frame it.** "FlavorPrint takes any recipe and a claimed cuisine and asks: does this recipe look like that
cuisine in this lab's terms? It uses the same aliaser, flavor network and fidelity scorer as our study."

**0:20–1:05 · A genuine Korean recipe.** Example: *{{ G.title }}*, claimed cuisine {{ G.claimed }}.
- Point at the four metrics: **Fidelity percentile {{ "%.0f"|format(G.fidelity_pct) }}** (compared with held-out
  genuine {{ G.claimed }} recipes), **Flavor sharing Ns {{ "%.1f"|format(G.ns) }}** (percentile {{ "%.0f"|format(G.ns_pct) }}),
  **Most likely cuisine {{ G.top5[0][0] }} ({{ "%.0f"|format(100 * G.top5[0][1]) }}%)**, **Ingredients matched {{ G.matched }}/{{ G.n_lines }}**.
{% if G.unmatched %}
- Open the **Ingredients** tab: "{{ G.unmatched|join('", "') }}" is not matched. "This is exactly the curation step:
  CulinaryDB has no entity for it yet, so a human adds the alias."
{% endif %}
- Open **Flavor sharing**: the histogram is real {{ G.claimed }} recipes, the line is this recipe; below is its flavor
  network. "Real {{ G.claimed }} cuisine has Z = {{ "%.0f"|format(G.z_real) }} against the random null:
  {{ "contrasting" if G.z_real < 0 else "uniform" }} food pairing, as in Singh & Bagler 2018."

**1:05–1:55 · The same app, an off-cuisine recipe claimed as Korean.** Select the **{{ O.example }}** example
(*{{ O.title }}*), then change **Claimed cuisine** to **{{ O.claimed }}**.
- **Fidelity percentile drops to {{ "%.0f"|format(O.fidelity_pct) }}**; most likely cuisine **{{ O.top5[0][0] }}
  ({{ "%.0f"|format(100 * O.top5[0][1]) }}%)**.
- **Suggestions** tab: unusual for {{ O.claimed }}: **{{ O.atypical|join(", ") if O.atypical else "none" }}**; flavor sharing
  is **{{ O.ns_verdict }}** relative to real {{ O.claimed }} recipes (Ns {{ "%.1f"|format(O.ns) }}, percentile
  {{ "%.0f"|format(O.ns_pct) }}). The score-component bars are all below zero.
- Say: "This is the check we apply to every LLM recipe, and the feedback is what our critique-and-revise steering
  sends back to the model."

**1:55–2:30 · The stereotype flag.** Select the **{{ S.example }}** example (*{{ S.title }}*, claimed {{ S.claimed }}).
- It uses **{{ S.signature_used|length }} of the cuisine's top-10 signature ingredients**; only
  {{ "%.0f"|format(S.signature_share_at_most) }}% of real recipes use this many or fewer, so the
  **stereotype flag {{ "appears" if S.stereotype_flag else "does not appear" }}**{% if demo.stereotype_cut_percent %} (cut-off: {{ demo.stereotype_cut_percent }}%){% endif %}.
- Say: "A recipe can be perfectly 'Indian' and still be a caricature: this is what our Stereotype Amplification Index
  measures at the level of a whole cuisine."

**2:30–2:50 · Dashboard tab.** Model × region food-pairing Z (same data as F3) and the scorecard medians.

**2:50–3:00 · Close.** "Everything you saw is in the FlavorAudit library: the same audit runs on a CSV of recipes with
`flavoraudit audit recipes.csv --cuisine-col cuisine --out out/`." Switch back to the slides.
